# =============================================================================
# lazyit — production-like / self-hosted environment.  See ADR-0028 (secrets).
#
#   cp infra/env/.env.prod.example infra/env/.env.prod   # then fill in CHANGE_ME values
#   chmod 600 infra/env/.env.prod                         # protect it on the host
#
# .env.prod is GITIGNORED. NEVER commit real secrets. Replace EVERY "CHANGE_ME".
# Bring the stack up with (from the repo root) — --env-file is required (the `environment:` blocks
# use ${VAR} interpolation resolved by Compose at parse time):
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f infra/docker-compose.prod.yaml \
#     --profile prod --env-file infra/env/.env.prod up -d --build
# =============================================================================

# --- PostgreSQL (internal network only — its port is never published) -------
# Generate a strong password: openssl rand -hex 24   (or: tr -dc A-Za-z0-9 </dev/urandom | head -c 32)
# IMPORTANT — use a URL-SAFE generator: this value is embedded verbatim in DATABASE_URL below, so it
# must contain none of  / : @ ? #  . A base64 '/' (e.g. `openssl rand -base64 24`) terminates the URL
# authority early and makes Prisma fail at migrate time with "P1013: invalid port number in database
# URL". hex (or the tr alphanumeric form) is safe. Keep this and DATABASE_URL's password IDENTICAL.
# NEVER reuse a personal/weak password — this file is the master key to all data.
POSTGRES_USER=lazyit
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_STRONG_PASSWORD
POSTGRES_DB=lazyit

# --- API (NestJS) -----------------------------------------------------------
# Must point at the `db` service host and reuse the Postgres credentials above.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://lazyit:CHANGE_ME_STRONG_PASSWORD@db:5432/lazyit?schema=public
PORT=3001
# Express `trust proxy` hop count (SEC-010). Behind Caddy the API has exactly ONE proxy hop, so set
# "1": `req.ip` then resolves to the VERIFIED client Caddy reports (Caddy's `trusted_proxies` drops a
# forged X-Forwarded-For from the public caller). This keys the /config/setup rate-limiter + the
# first-run audit IP. Leave UNSET only if the API is reached directly with no reverse proxy.
TRUST_PROXY=1
# Public site URL (the origin the browser uses). Local prod-like default below;
# for a real domain use https://lazyit.example.com (no trailing slash).
# Also used as Auth.js AUTH_URL (the web compose maps it) to pin the OIDC redirect_uri
# (<WEB_ORIGIN>/api/auth/callback/oidc — the redirect URI you register in your IdP for BYOI).
# LAN mode (ADR-0087): LEAVE UNSET (comment it out). With no fixed origin the api reflects the request
# Origin and the web derives its base URL from the Host — so a DHCP IP change needs no re-pin.
WEB_ORIGIN=https://localhost:8443
# AUTH_TRUST_HOST (ADR-0087) — set to "true" ONLY in host-agnostic LAN mode (WEB_ORIGIN unset). It tells
# the api to reflect the request Origin for CORS and the web to trust the forwarded Host (NextAuth
# trustHost). Safe ONLY because api/web sit behind Caddy on a private network — NEVER expose the app
# containers directly with this on. Leave UNSET (commented) for local/real (the pinned WEB_ORIGIN wins).
# AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true
# Max upload size (MB) for the article import endpoint. Default 5 if unset.
MAX_IMPORT_SIZE_MB=5

# --- Authentication mode (ADR-0086) -----------------------------------------
# EXPLICIT-REQUIRED, three-state, IMMUTABLE per instance — one of: local | oidc  (shim is dev-only and is
# REFUSED under NODE_ENV=production). There is NO implicit default: an UNSET AUTH_MODE is a hard boot
# failure. UPGRADE NOTE: a deployment whose .env.prod predates this line and relied on the old
# "unset ⇒ OIDC" behaviour MUST add `AUTH_MODE=oidc` by hand BEFORE upgrading — otherwise the API refuses
# to start (this is intentional: the alternative was a silent flip to `local` → outage). An existing OIDC
# deploy that sets it needs ZERO data migration.
#   - local — first-party auth (no external IdP): lazyit owns username/email + password. Requires
#             SESSION_SIGNING_SECRET below. The default (ADR-0086).
#   - oidc  — your own OIDC IdP (BYOI, ADR-0102 — there is no bundled IdP). Requires the OIDC client
#             block below (OIDC_ISSUER + OIDC_JWKS_URI at least).
# The mode is chosen ONCE and cannot be changed on a populated instance (a persisted marker refuses to
# boot on a mismatch — migrating modes is out-of-band).
# NETWORK NOTE (ADR-0087): host-agnostic LAN mode (LAZYIT_SITE_ADDRESS=:80 + AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true) REQUIRES
# AUTH_MODE=local — an OIDC redirect URI is registered against one fixed origin and cannot be host-agnostic.
AUTH_MODE=local
# SESSION_SIGNING_SECRET — REQUIRED only when AUTH_MODE=local. The persistent HMAC key for the first-party
# session token (HS256, ADR-0086 §4). At least 32 chars; generate with: openssl rand -hex 32. DISTINCT from
# AUTH_SECRET (the web's Auth.js cookie key). Lower DR severity than WORKFLOW_SECRET_KEY —
# rotating it only forces re-login (no data loss). NEVER per-boot-random. Leave UNSET in oidc mode.
# SESSION_SIGNING_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_OPENSSL_RAND_HEX_32

# --- Valkey (ADR-0053) — BullMQ broker for async workers; internal service, never published -----
# Redis-protocol URL. Points at the `valkey` service on the internal network (port 6379 is never
# exposed to the host, like the DBs — SEC-005/ADR-0028).
# REQUIRED. If UNSET the API falls back to redis://127.0.0.1:6379 — inside the api container that is
# the container itself (no Valkey there), so every async import fails. The API no longer floods: it
# logs the resolved URL (password redacted) at boot, bounds reconnection, and returns a clean 503 on
# import instead of hanging (#257) — but you still must set this to redis://valkey:6379.
# UPGRADE NOTE: a deployment whose infra/env/.env.prod predates this line MUST add it by hand (the
# guided start.sh only writes it on a FRESH render; on an existing .env.prod it appends only its allowlisted
# generated keys, never this one), then recreate
# the api container:
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f infra/docker-compose.prod.yaml --profile prod \
#     --env-file infra/env/.env.prod up -d api
REDIS_URL=redis://valkey:6379

# --- Applications Workflow Engine secrets (ADR-0054) -----------------------
# AES-256-GCM master key for the engine's OWN encrypted credential store (WorkflowSecret) — the per-app
# connector credentials. EXACTLY 32 bytes; generate with: openssl rand -hex 32  (→ 64 hex chars).
# A SECRET — never logged, never committed. The recovery linchpin alongside POSTGRES_PASSWORD: add it to
# the backup runbook. Losing it = losing every stored connector credential
# (recoverable only by re-entering them). The API FAILS LOUD AT BOOT if the engine is enabled and this
# is missing / the wrong length.
WORKFLOW_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE_ME_OPENSSL_RAND_HEX_32

# --- Instance SMTP / outbound email (ADR-0079) -----------------------------
# AES-256-GCM master key for the instance SMTP PASSWORD at rest (Settings → Instance → SMTP). Its OWN key
# axis, SEPARATE from WORKFLOW_SECRET_KEY ("one key per subsystem") — never reuse the same value for both.
# EXACTLY 32 bytes; generate with: openssl rand -hex 32  (→ 64 hex chars). A SECRET — never logged.
# OPTIONAL at boot (unlike WORKFLOW_SECRET_KEY): the API starts fine without it and an UNAUTHENTICATED
# relay keeps working — but saving an SMTP PASSWORD returns a clean 409 and stores NOTHING until it is set.
# NOT a DR linchpin: losing it costs one SMTP password, re-typed by an admin (docs/05-runbooks/backups.md).
# UPGRADE NOTE: a .env.prod written before this line has no key. Re-running `./infra/start.sh` on the
# existing install (the `git pull` + start.sh upgrade path) APPENDS one for you — any auth mode, backup
# first, existing lines untouched (ADR-0047 amendment 2026-09-26); `--reconfigure` also adds it.
# infra/update.sh never edits this file and stops on the missing key; append it by hand and recreate the
# api container:
#   printf 'SMTP_SECRET_KEY=%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" >> infra/env/.env.prod
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f infra/docker-compose.prod.yaml --profile prod \
#     --env-file infra/env/.env.prod up -d api
# An ALREADY-PRESENT key must never be regenerated — it decrypts the SMTP password already stored.
SMTP_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE_ME_OPENSSL_RAND_HEX_32

# --- AI assistant (ADR-0097) — OPTIONAL, off until an admin enables it in Settings -> AI ----------
# AES-256-GCM master key for the AI provider API KEY at rest (Settings -> AI). Its OWN key axis, SEPARATE
# from WORKFLOW_SECRET_KEY and SMTP_SECRET_KEY ("one key per subsystem") — never reuse another key's value.
# EXACTLY 32 bytes; generate with: openssl rand -hex 32  (→ 64 hex chars). A SECRET — never logged.
# OPTIONAL at boot: the API starts and behaves exactly as without the AI feature when it is unset; saving a
# provider API key returns a clean 409 and stores NOTHING until it is set (a keyless provider, such as a
# local Ollama, and the MCP server work without it). NOT a DR linchpin: losing it costs one API key,
# re-entered by an admin (docs/05-runbooks/backups.md).
# Shipped COMMENTED on purpose — unlike SMTP_SECRET_KEY above — so the guided update (infra/update.sh),
# which stops on any ACTIVE key missing from your .env.prod, never stops an instance that does not use
# AI. A guided install (infra/start.sh), `./infra/start.sh --reconfigure`, and re-running
# `./infra/start.sh` on an existing install that lacks it (ADR-0047 amendment 2026-09-26) all write it for
# you, ACTIVE; to add it by hand, then recreate the api container:
#   grep -q '^AI_SECRET_KEY=' infra/env/.env.prod \
#     || printf 'AI_SECRET_KEY=%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" >> infra/env/.env.prod
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f infra/docker-compose.prod.yaml --profile prod \
#     --env-file infra/env/.env.prod up -d api
# An ALREADY-PRESENT key must never be regenerated — it decrypts the provider API key already stored.
# AI_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE_ME_OPENSSL_RAND_HEX_32
# AI_WORKER_CONCURRENCY — OPTIONAL, default 4 when unset (invalid values also fall back to 4; capped at 16).
# How many AI runs (chat turns and headless prompts) the api container executes at once. Runs are I/O-bound
# (waiting on the provider), so raise it only with CPU/memory headroom. Nothing ever writes it for you —
# leave it commented unless you want a different value.
# AI_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=4

# --- Directory sync (on-prem AD/LDAP, ADR-0091) — OPTIONAL, off until an admin enables it ------------
# AES-256-GCM master key for the LDAP BIND PASSWORD at rest (Settings -> Instance -> Directory sync). Its
# OWN key axis, SEPARATE from WORKFLOW_SECRET_KEY, SMTP_SECRET_KEY and AI_SECRET_KEY ("one key per
# subsystem") — never reuse another key's value. EXACTLY 32 bytes; generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# (→ 64 hex chars). A SECRET — never logged.
# OPTIONAL at boot: the API starts and behaves exactly as before when it is unset. But saving a directory
# connection WITH a bind password returns a clean 409 and stores NOTHING — not even the other fields —
# until it is set. NOT a DR linchpin: losing it costs one bind password, re-typed by an admin
# (docs/05-runbooks/backups.md).
# Shipped COMMENTED on purpose, like AI_SECRET_KEY, so the guided update (infra/update.sh), which stops on
# any ACTIVE key missing from your .env.prod, never stops an instance that does not sync a directory. A
# guided install (infra/start.sh), `./infra/start.sh --reconfigure`, and re-running `./infra/start.sh` on an
# existing install that lacks it (ADR-0047 amendment 2026-09-26) all write it for you, ACTIVE; to add it by
# hand, then recreate the api container:
#   grep -q '^DIRECTORY_SECRET_KEY=' infra/env/.env.prod \
#     || printf 'DIRECTORY_SECRET_KEY=%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" >> infra/env/.env.prod
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f infra/docker-compose.prod.yaml --profile prod \
#     --env-file infra/env/.env.prod up -d api
# An ALREADY-PRESENT key must never be regenerated — it decrypts the bind password already stored.
# DIRECTORY_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE_ME_OPENSSL_RAND_HEX_32

# --- Caddy reverse proxy / TLS ---------------------------------------------
# The Caddy site address. Three shapes (ADR-0026, ADR-0087):
#   - "localhost"          -> Caddy internal CA HTTPS (browser warns until the CA is trusted). [local]
#   - "lazyit.example.com" -> your FQDN, publicly reachable on :80/:443 for Let's Encrypt.      [real]
#   - ":80" (PORT-ONLY)    -> plain HTTP for ANY Host on that port, NO TLS, no cert, no redirect. [lan]
# The port-only ":80" form is what makes a host-agnostic LAN deploy work: Caddy answers regardless of
# the Host/IP the browser used, so a DHCP IP change needs no re-pin (verified with `caddy validate`).
# Set it together with AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true + WEB_ORIGIN unset (the lan contract).
LAZYIT_SITE_ADDRESS=localhost
# ACME contact email for Let's Encrypt on a real domain (also uncomment `email` in the Caddyfile).
# LAZYIT_TLS_EMAIL=ops@example.com

# Host ports Caddy publishes (high ports so they never clash with dev 3000/3001/5432).
LAZYIT_HTTP_PORT=8080
LAZYIT_HTTPS_PORT=8443

# --- Meilisearch (ADR-0035) — internal service; never publish port 7700 externally --------
MEILI_HOST=http://meilisearch:7700
MEILI_MASTER_KEY=CHANGE_ME_STRONG_MASTER_KEY
MEILI_ENV=production
MEILI_NO_ANALYTICS=true

# --- OIDC client (BYOI, ADR-0102) — only when AUTH_MODE=oidc -------------------------------------
# Your own OIDC IdP (Entra ID, Okta, Keycloak, Authentik …): register a web client whose redirect URI is
# <WEB_ORIGIN>/api/auth/callback/oidc, then UNCOMMENT and set every key below. The guided install
# (infra/start.sh, answer 'byoi') writes them for you. Every key stays COMMENTED here on purpose: the guided
# update (infra/update.sh) stops on any ACTIVE key missing from your .env.prod, and a local-auth instance
# has none of these. IDENTITY_PROVIDER_TYPE is not needed — unset means generic OIDC.
# API — token validation:
# OIDC_ISSUER=https://login.example.com
# OIDC_JWKS_URI is the `jwks_uri` value of <OIDC_ISSUER>/.well-known/openid-configuration.
# OIDC_JWKS_URI=https://login.example.com/oauth2/keys
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=CHANGE_ME
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME
# Web (Auth.js) — the same issuer and client:
# AUTH_ISSUER=https://login.example.com
# AUTH_CLIENT_ID=CHANGE_ME
# AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME

# Auth.js session secret — encrypts the session JWT cookie. MANDATORY.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 33
# AUTH_TRUST_HOST is set to "true" in the compose (web runs behind Caddy — ADR-0039).
AUTH_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_RANDOM_33_BYTES_BASE64

# --- Automated backups (OPT-IN sidecar) — see docs/05-runbooks/backups.md ---
# The `backup` profile service runs cron + pg_dump for BOTH databases to host ./backups, then
# prunes old dumps. Disabled unless you start it (it needs BOTH the prod and backup profiles):
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f infra/docker-compose.prod.yaml --env-file infra/env/.env.prod \
#     --profile prod --profile backup up -d backup
# All optional (defaults shown). The sidecar does NOT back up THIS file — copy it
# off-host yourself; it is the DR linchpin (holds WORKFLOW_SECRET_KEY).
# BACKUP_CRON=30 2 * * *        # crontab syntax. Default: daily at 02:30.
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14      # prune dumps older than N days. Default: 14.
# Optional offsite copy — OFF unless set. Runs once per dump with the dump path as "$1".
# Stays local-disk-only by default (no mandatory cloud — ADR-0028). Example with rclone:
# BACKUP_OFFSITE_CMD=rclone copy "$1" offsite:lazyit/
# Optional failure alert hook — OFF unless set. Runs when a dump fails (non-zero pg_dump, or an
# empty/unrestorable dump) or the offsite copy fails, with a one-line reason as "$1". No built-in
# vendor integration (ADR-0028 "no mandatory cloud"). A failed run is ALWAYS loud in the logs
# ("[lazyit-backup] FAILED ...") and exits non-zero whether or not this is set.
# BACKUP_ON_FAILURE_CMD=curl -fsS -d "$1" https://hooks.example/lazyit-backup
